  "notes": {
    "novatek/nt36536-xn242.bin": "the vendor keeps the touch application firmware as a property inside a dtbo overlay (nt36536@0/firmware-data-0); the driver loses it on every panel prepare and requests it again, so the installed root has to carry it",
    "qca/gngbtfw20.mbn": "what mainline hci_qca asks a WCN7860 for (qca/gngbtfw%02x.mbn plus a gngbtnv name built from the board id). Confirmed on this unit on 2026-09-22, once BT_EN was wired to the right PMIC: the driver requests exactly these two names and the controller comes up on them -- \"QCA Downloading qca/gngbtfw20.mbn\", then \"qca/gngbtnv20.bin\", then \"QCA FW build version: BTFW.GANGES.2.0.0-00440-PATCHZ-1_DEF\". No board-id variant is needed: btqca reads this unit's board id as 0 and so asks for the plain gngbtnv20.bin rather than a gngbtnv20.b<id>. The stock stack uses a different set entirely -- brhbtfw10.tlv and brhbtnv10.b* in bluetooth.img -- which this port does not use.",
    "qcom/sm8750/gen80000_zap.mbn": "the linux-firmware zap, signed with the SecTools test root for Qualcomm reference devices; this retail unit's TZ rejects it. Kept because a unit whose TZ accepts the test root needs it. qcom/sm8750/erhai/gen80000_zap.mbn is the same shader signed for this product",
    "qcom/sm8750/erhai/": "the ADSP PIL set is per-firmware-build; it is the modem_a content of the ColorOS build this tablet runs, and the DT points remoteproc at adsp.mdt and adsp_dtb.mdt under this directory",
    "awinic/erhai/aw88261_acf.bin": "Unmodified /odm/firmware/aw882xx_acf.bin, verified against the running stock system. AW88261 V0 ACF; the codec firmware-name must select this board-specific path. Channel profiles are ROM data, not per-unit factory calibration. The kernel DT selects it for all six amplifiers; their DVDD supply is still a placeholder and playback is unverified.",
    "qcom/sm8750/erhai/vpu35_4v.mbn": "Unmodified /vendor/firmware/vpu35_4v.mbn, the signed Iris (VPU 3.5) video core image of this ColorOS build. Upstream linux-firmware has no SM8750 Iris image, and a retail unit's TZ only authenticates the vendor-signed one, so the DT names this board-specific path with firmware-name. Staged for the video decoder bring-up; the iris node is not yet enabled in the shipped DT and decoding is unverified.",
    "qcom/sm8750/erhai/gen80000_zap.mbn": "Unmodified /vendor/firmware/gen80000_zap.mbn of this ColorOS build. Its code segments are byte-identical to the linux-firmware zap; only the hash segment and signature differ: it chains to OPLUS ROOT CA 1, the root this unit's TZ already accepts for the ADSP image in the same directory. It replaces the test-signed zap on this unit only, selected by the gpu zap-shader node's firmware-name once the DT enables it; the shipped DT keeps that node disabled and loading it is unverified",
    "ath12k/WCN7860/hw2.0/erhai/": "Board data candidate from this product's own ROM, next to the ROCKNIX set rather than over it. ath12k builds every board-* path from the fixed ath12k/<fw.dir>/ directory and firmware-name does not apply to board files, so the driver never reads this directory: switching means installing these two files at ath12k/WCN7860/hw2.0/board-2.bin and regdb.bin in place of the ROCKNIX pair, which erhai-firmware owns outright. board-2.bin is /odm/etc/wifi/peach/bdwlan.elf and regdb.bin packed by ath12k-bdencoder under the two names ROCKNIX's board-2.bin uses, the first of which is the name this unit's ath12k asks for (board_id 0xff); the stock Android WLAN driver loads the same two files from /odm/etc/wifi ahead of the modem partition. Radio behaviour is unverified"
  },
